## 8. Open Issues

The following define the research and specification agenda for the payment interface. They represent the boundary of the current specification and the forward work programme.

**Cross-border payment and Exchange federation**
When buyer and seller use different national Taler Exchange instances, the Contract Terms must reference a resolvable Exchange endpoint across jurisdictions. The cross-Exchange payment reference verification protocol requires a federation model analogous to the open issue in [signing-layer.md](signing-layer.md).

**Merchant integration API**
The supplier (seller) must be able to verify a received payment against a specific M0 without disclosure of buyer identity. The merchant API specification — how the supplier receives payment confirmation from Exchange — requires separate specification in coordination with the Taler merchant API team.

**Taler wallet B2B UX**
Current Taler wallet implementations are optimised for B2C micropayments. B2B invoice payment with amounts in thousands of euros and deadlines in days or weeks requires a specific UX flow distinct from standard Taler checkout. A reference UX specification for the B2B context is a contribution to the Taler wallet ecosystem.

**PSD2 and national payment law compliance**
PSD2 and national payment legislation define requirements for B2B payments above certain thresholds. Compatibility of Taler coin mechanics with these requirements across Member States requires legal analysis before operational deployment.

**Contract Terms standardisation**
The Contract Terms binding defined in Section 4 is a DTEP-F proposal. Standardisation of EN 16931 invoice fields as Taler Contract Terms parameters — particularly the scrubbed merchant and product fields — requires coordination with the Taler core team and potential extension of the Contract Terms schema.

